-- lib/work.cub — the work's graph, as data of the language (2026-11-12), over
-- lib/prelude.cub's List. Nothing here is specific to this repository: a particular work declares its nodes as a data
-- type and the relations below over it (this repository's: work/graph.cub).
--
import "core.cub"
-- t64 (2026-11-12): The work as one graph of nodes. Done 2026-11-12: goals and tickets are one
-- kind of node (work/graph.cub; no kind enum, no ticket record, closed nodes leave the state); every
-- declaration of the code is a node of the same graph, its reason computed from the machine's
-- reading `#uses` (a declaration with a reason mentions it), from the machine naming it (src/), or
-- stated in the comment above it as `(part of NODE)` or `(prerequisite to NODE)` (tools/work.py why,
-- unexplained). Measured 2026-11-12 over LIB and the work's files: 1586 of 2504 declarations have
-- no reason, 393 of them used by nothing; stating those is the work that remains of point 1. The
-- reading covers every file (each file no file imports loaded alone with its imports, t30). Point 10:
-- `work.py open` opens a node with its text at its site, and asks y/N when the node it is part of
-- is the root or a part of the root. Not yet done: breakdown claims checked (point 11).
-- One graph, not two (the user, 2026-11-12): reasons for existence and goals are the same thing, not
-- separate pieces of work. Every top-level declaration of the work, code included, is a node of the
-- one graph: its reason is that something with a reason uses it (computed, by key) or that it is
-- required for a node (stated); a goal or a ticket is a node like any other. Rebuilding this record
-- is rebuilding that graph, and point 1 below is part of it, not a separate ticket.
-- The user's direction for the rebuild (2026-11-12), every point as given:
-- 1. Reasons for existence. Every top-level object in the code is used somewhere or states its
--    purpose for being in the codebase: "what justifies you being here". Where use is implicit (the
--    object is used by something else) nothing is written; otherwise the reason is stated, and the
--    "why" data is required, so nothing exists without a documented reason indicating its payoff.
--    Wanted for a bird's-eye view: navigating the work to understand the state of what is there and
--    why. It is the same relation as goals, more broadly reasons for doing things.
-- 2. Generated terms. A generator sets the reason of each term at the time it generates it; no
--    further notion of a group of terms is needed.
-- 3. One noun. Not tickets, goals and claims: one kind of node.
-- 4. The number of things stamped directly onto a top-level node must never exceed a handful: there
--    is no reason for many terms used nowhere and attached straight to "the core".
-- 5. Names say what they claim. "Serves core" is not a meaningful name; "prerequisite to core
--    completion" is clearer. Agents can still invent reasons, but a clear name gets a false one
--    caught in any given pass ("why would this be a prerequisite? no way") where a vague one is let
--    through ("maybe it serves the core somehow").
-- 6. Specificity. Claiming that some small thing is needed "because it is essential to the whole
--    project" is costly and harmful. There must be systemic friction pushing every attachment to the
--    most specific node available.
-- 7. Fixed breakdowns. Sub-nodes under the core can be defined and frozen, so no further nodes are
--    added there. Being unable to freeze them means the exact breakdown of what determines core
--    correctness and completion is not known; it has been defined quite clearly.
-- 8. Attaching high must feel like changing the org chart, not annotating locally: "annotate this
--    serves core and move on" and "add a fourth near-top-level category that everyone sees when they
--    look" are different actions, and the lever should be the second when that is what it does. The
--    norm is to be critical of the central organization looking sloppy or disproportionate.
-- 9. A missed requirement is a class. If some small box outside the determined criteria turns out
--    to be needed, that suggests a whole class of them: not a small ticket to fix this one, but a
--    major new child of core completeness, "correctly determine everything core completeness
--    requires and fill every gap", because the prior belief in the top-level breakdown is refuted.
-- 10. Visibility. Taste calibration should be enough if higher levels are far more visible: putting
--    something at a high level is putting it in front of everyone, using a scarce commons, publicly.
--    How such norms get established is open; when attaching through the command line, it can ask
--    for confirmation (y/N), saying that it is essential to give the most specific node, and that
--    this puts the new node on the same visibility level as the other sub-goals.
-- 11. The model. Dependence between goals is a binary fact. Depth below the root (strata) determines
--    visibility across interfaces, so situating a node at a visibility level is part of what the
--    record says. Adding a small thing at a high level implies a bigger issue: not the small thing
--    itself, but that a new node had to be opened at level n because level n−1's breakdown is
--    invalidated. Each level is fixed relative to the level above: at every point, the children of a
--    node are believed to be all the sub-goals whose completion together achieves it, and that
--    invariant is maintained. A finding to the contrary (the level-n children do not suffice for
--    their parent at n−1) means another node is needed at level n, and refutes the belief that
--    everything required is known.
-- 12. Validity. Having specified everything required is trivial from the start: leaf nodes can be as
--    coarse as wanted. "Do the project" <- "figure out how to do the project" is a valid statement
--    of everything required; "do the project" <- "implement xyz" is not. Expressed uncertainty gives
--    validity: the only invalid state is expressed certainty of sufficiency where it is insufficient.
-- From these: a node holds what it is required for, and a claim that its children suffice (certain,
-- or expressly uncertain through a child like "determine what X requires"); its depth is its
-- visibility; the record is valid while no certain claim is known false. Applied 2026-11-12: the
-- core's breakdown into core-correct, partial-evaluation and native is what the paper states
-- (certain); the breakdown under core-correct is a collection of findings and is to carry an
-- uncertain child until the rules are read against the published calculus; the root is made of
-- parts that each deliver (each-of), so its claim is open by nature; t42–t44 were wrongly attached
-- to core-correct (efficiency of the knowledge system, not correctness of the core) and no longer
-- are.
--
--
-- A node is `node id parts value breakdown`; `leaf id parts` is a node with no parts of its own. The
-- id word is its identity (two nodes with the same parts are still two nodes) until text can be
-- written as a term (t63) and a node's name can be its identity. The relations, and nothing else:
--   part-of n    the nodes n is a part of. Where the whole is all-of, n is a prerequisite to it; where
--                it is each-of, n is a part that delivers on its own. A node that is part of nothing
--                is a root and is declared one (roots); a node part of nothing and not declared, and a
--                cycle, are errors (tools/work.py check).
--   value n      how n's value depends on its parts: all-of (nothing until every part is complete: a
--                companion repository with half a core is not one) or each-of (each completed part
--                delivers).
--   breakdown n  whether n's parts are claimed to suffice for it: sufficient, or not-known. Claiming
--                sufficient where it is not is the one invalid state; not-known is always valid.
--   after        pairs (n , ms): what must be complete before n can be done.
--   speeds       pairs (n , ms): the nodes whose work n makes faster when it lands.
-- A node's text is its comment: a goal's above its part-of clause, a node opened for a specific piece
-- of work (tN) at the place in the code it concerns, as `tN (YYYY-MM-DD):`. Every node in the state is
-- open; closing one removes it, and the history keeps it.
-- Priority is not stored: it is computed from these (tools/work.py priority, until it is a term,
-- t37). A piece of work's value lands when it and everything its value is gated on are complete.
-- Work whose value is gated on incomplete work comes after that work even when nothing stops it being
-- started; among the parts of one all-of node, what speeds other work comes first; the total time is
-- the same in any order, so the order only moves when value lands.
data Combine = all-of | each-of
data Breakdown = sufficient | not-known
data Node = node (id : Word) (part-of : List Node) (value : Combine) (breakdown : Breakdown)
leaf : Word → List Node → Node
leaf i ps = node i ps all-of not-known
